>But *UNIX* is available from zillions of vendors. 
 
Wrong.  Unix is available from one vendor.  What there are zillions of is
O/S's based on Unix.

> And apps are portable (like Cnews, emacs, g++ etc)with simple 'configure'
> scripts.          

Some apps are more portable than others.  Currently the most portable apps
are those that are Posix compliant.  This means Unix-derived O/S's
are not at any portability advantage, as plenty of non-Unix-derived O/S's
are Posix compliant, and not all Unix-derived ones are.
                                  
>There is even binary portability among some processor families
>(Inter binary interface ?) that enables me to run my app on SCO
>solaris univel and others. 

>This discussion is a little silly anyway, comparing a closed
>propertary os and and open widly available one.

Which are also proprietary.  Can people *please* stop muddying such
discussions by prefixing this useless word.  Rather than make any useful
point, all you end up doing is creating tautology.

And why is the former "closed" and the letter "open"?  Do I take it you
agree with Channing's definition of these terms?  I repeat my assertion
that his definition (an "open" O/S is one available from multiple vendors
to run on multiple hardware) is totally useless.  I also repeat my
challenge for anyone to demonstrate a scenario when such a O/S would be of
any benefit.  Note that such a scenario would have to be based on a
specific multi-vendor O/S (I can't think of a single example).


