qualifier, I get an AXP GAWK executable that compiles without errors,
yet produces completely incorrect results when run.


	>Remember that the compiler is an ANSI C compiler, and people
	>bemoaned that VAXC wasn't.  "Real soon now" DEC C should be
	>available on the VAX, which should help this out.

Yes, VAX C has a lot of limitations (or so I hear).  I expect that the
VAX C to DEC C migration on the VAXen is going to be a little trying
at times as well.  At least DEC is finally fixing its VMS C compiler.


	>	That there are even different C standards is what is
	>causing you problems, and can in the Unix space as well...

I didn't say it wouldn't cause trouble on a Unix system.  A different
author (Michael Lemke) wrote:

		# And mind you, even the program you compiled in 1981
		# still runs on the latest VMS version on an AXP
		# (Alpha).

My reply was simply that this isn't necessarily true.  Yes, the
>VESTed< version runs, but any attempt to compile the same program
under DEC C on the AXPs breaks (even with /STAN=VAXC).

Are all VAX executables which are run through VEST >guaranteed< to
work on the AXPs?


	>> The fact is that even on VMS moving programs between
	>> architectures can be as hairy as Unix at times.

	>	But you can get it working in short order, if anything
	>using VEST.  There isn't anything in the Unix space that
	>compares to that.

Yes, VEST is a nice utility to provide a temporary solution to running
your VAX program on an AXP.  But it is >only< a temporary solution, in
my humble opinion.  (See a previous article by Benjamin Goldsteen for
a similar view.)

[Note that some other companies are doing similar things in
architecture transitions.  Apple is reported to have some sort of
Motorola 680x0 interpreter on their new PowerPC machines (to be release some
time next Spring).  Hopefully this and DEC's VEST will be a migration
trend for the future....]
