|>Touche!
|>
|>But my point still holds.  DEC claiming that VMS is "open" and then
|>using the "multiple architecture" point is rather misleading.  Whether
|>it is two or three or ten architectures, all are made by DEC, to my
|>knowledge.  That is like claiming that the Macintosh is "open" because
|>it runs on multiple architectures (Motorola and PowerPC).  Carl, how
|>many architecures >is< VMS supported on?  The fact is, right now you can
|>only run the OS on hardware sold by the company, locking one into
|>buying from one company.

Thats not the point about open systems. A system is open if it interfaces
to other systems. Vendor multiplicity does not really come into it. You
can only buy Postscript from Adobe, does that make it closed? You can only
buy Microsoft Word from Microsoft and so on. However Microsoft Word is
open in so far as it allows you to import and export files in a wide variety of
formats. This makes it possible to use the product in conjunction with
almost any other. 

VMS runs on VAX and AXP. Both technologies are avaliable for license from DEC
(I presume that since they license AXP and have licensed VAX in the past
it is still posible to license VAX but why anyone would want to...). There
will be second sources for AXP chips sometime next year or the year after.

Is it really necessary for a software product to run on any machine to 
be open? there are very great advantages to adapting the hardware to its
function as has been done with VAX. The AXP solution of allowing adaptation
under software control is both elegant and powerful.

|>And the original point still holds: "Unix" has so many different
|>variants because it runs on so many different platforms (many which
|>are >very< different from each other).  (Note: I am not claiming that
|>it is "open" -- that is all useless marketing hype.)  There are going
|>to be a lot of variations in the different types of "Unixes" out
|>there, like this whole thread about "ll" and "ls -d".  That
|>(fortunately or unfortunately) is a given.  VMS benefits (and suffers)
|>from tight control by DEC, which has tried to instill a lot of
|>consistency in its design.

The plethora of standards was not inevitable. The problem was that UNIX
was not worked on centrally until too late.


|>If you want to compare Unix to VMS, it is best done on a level field.
|>Let's compare AT&T System V (produced >BY< AT&T) on the platforms it
|>was supported.  We will consider only Unixes directly created and
|>supported by AT&T, not those created and supported by the zillions of
|>other vendors who picked up on Unix.  Let's see how inconstent AT&T's
|>Unix is, and compare this to VMS' inconsistencies.

The analogy is not accurate. The problems with consistency in UNIX are
not entirely between variants. Lack of overall coherence is a very major
problem. A lot of cooks have been making that broth, mostly grad students
by the looks of things. Much of what we call UNIX predates it - such as TCP/IP
for example. This makes it hardly surprising that there are inconsistencies.

|>(Note that I know very little about AT&T System V Unix, so it may be
|>very inconsistent, but it may not.  Anybody have proof?)

It depends on what you mean by consistency. At the shell level the commands
have the usual plethora of obscure flags. The problem is not consistency
but predictability though ie:

If I know operation X and want to do a similar operation Y can I predict from
the behaviour of the commands that do X how to achieve Y?

Systems that are predictable and can be easily comprehended are I beleive ones
that have good design.


It is certainly a mistake to assume that because many users use a product
that it is well designed. The standards trap means that once a standard has
become established it is difficult to change it even when it is obvious that
there is a better solution. Take for example the difficulty of making 
everyone drive on the left of the road despite the fact that most countries
and the majority of the worlds population do so already.

The most popular and successful operating systems of the past twenty years
have been first CPM and then its sucessor, MSDOS. I doubt that many would
consider MSDOS to be a suitable operating system for a machine as powerful
as a PC with a Pentium and 64Mb of memory.


UNIX has undoubtedly been sucessful and given time can be used to do almost
anything. In many cases though there are serious difficulaties with it. In
the area of Real time and asynchronous handling there are serious difficulties.
It is certainly not a good idea to take an arbitrary UNIX and attempt to use
it for that purpose. Not all are up to it. Experience has taught me that
many of the socket libraries are chronicaly inefficient and some are even
buggy. Real Time I/O is an area where VMS is very strong and the cost of 
producing a system using it is likely to be substantially less than a UNIX
approach in many cases. High reliability systems and database processing is
another area where VMS is stronger than UNIX.


It is a big mistake to extrapolate from the desktop, single user environment
to the big wide world. Features that seem unimportant when you have one
person to a machine become much more critical with systems linking thousands
of people in a single cooperative task.


Despite my many criticisms of UNIX I do not consider it to be an entirely
negative influence.  In fact I was a very big advocate for it in certain
circumstances. I have considered the workstation the route of the future
since I first saw the VS2000 prove the posibilities. However today I think
that most of the expansion possibility has been exploited and some of the
major design principles are no longer as beneficial as they once were.

UNIX was technically superior to many (but not all) of the
systems it replaced. To consider it as many do to be the apex of computing
ability is a very narrow view. Many of the design decisions of UNIX
that make it easy to use in the single user type environment make it very
inflexible in the multi-user-cooperative environment. There is a big
difference between multiple users using a single machine for different and
separate tasks and using multiple machines to cooperate in a single task.
Having written systems type code for both purposes I can assure you that
the former is far easier than the latter.


If we are engineers then the first question that we should ask is cost. 
Is it cheaper in resources, both human and capital to achieve a result 
using UNIX or VMS? In some cases the answer will be a mixture. 


Standards are all very well. But remember that the only standard that is
adhered to is the one which is obsolete. This even applies to standards
as basic as ANSII which at first assumed that only north americans would need
to use computers, later it was realised that Europeans also wanted to be able
to spell names such as Bij�rn correctly. Today the idea is begining to
percolate through that Chineese, Arabs and Greeks may wish to communicate
their thoughts in their native language and alphabet.

Adhering to a standard that is not technically adequate is not a good
choice for an engineer. The correct approach then is to either find an
adequate standard or extend an existing one. Assuming that because 
something is a standard it must work is in my experience a very bad
mistake.

On the immediate question HP-UX or VMS ask yourself:-

1)	Including retraining costs (if any) which will be cheaper for
	you to install?
2)	Which is likely to be cheaper to administer?
3)	Which will allow the users to be maximally efficient?
4)	How long is it likely to be before a switch to another system
	becomes desirable?
5)	Would a reconfiguration of current systems such as use of
	DECwindows if not already doing so be a possible solution?

With the current state of WNT it can hardly be considered a solution for 
many people in production environments. But it may well be sooner than
you think. Consider whether a switch to HP-UX may close future options
of a switch to WNT.

Also remember that there is no point in banging your head against the
wall to force people to do something they don't want to. These days I
find that it is very much more beneficial to my health to give my advice
and if it is not taken find something else to do until such time as it
is.


--
Phillip M. Hallam-Baker

