In article <1993Oct12.022947.1992@debug.cuc.ab.ca>, root@debug.cuc.ab.ca (Robert Franke) writes:
> 
> Hello Vax experts,
> 
>     I recently purchased a MicroVax II.  

	Congrats!
>                                                 Just the box, no manuals,
> or instructions of any kind.

	Bummer...

>                                        It has a 190 meg hard drive, and
> a "Compactape" tape drive (TK50 I'm assuming).  Currently there is
                             ^^^^ most likely...
> VMS installed on the hard drive.  It boots fine (although it's 
> complaining about being away from it's DECherd and not having the
> 3 external hard drives it once had) and seems to be running without
> any problems.  I have a LOT of questions, as can be expected with a
> machine that has no documentation with it :).  I run SCO Unix, Sco Xenix,
> and work on various Unix platforms, but never VMS.  I can hack my way
> through most things, but this has got me at a stand still (lack of
> information of any kind).  If you have a moment, could you do me the
> favour of answering the following questions:
> 
> 1.) If you had a MicroVax II, what would you do with it?  What O/S
>     would you run, etc?

	VMS!  

> 2.) What CPU is in a MicroVax II?  How fast is it?  How many users
>     can it handle at one time?

	It is a CVAX-II microprocessor chip, and it runs about as fast
as a VAX 11/780 (About 1.0 SPECint89).

> 3.) What is the standard configuration of memory in a MicroVax II,
>     and how would I find out how much ram is in it?

	It depends.  You can get up to 16MB for it.

> 4.) Other than "b" for boot, what options do I have at the pre-boot
>     prompt ">>>" thing?

	Not much, just run diags and some tests...  You can also control
how the system boots, though...

> 7.) Currently the system is running VMS, but ofcourse, I have no
>     OPERATOR or SYSTEM passwords.  Is there an over-ride ability
>     that can be done from the main console? The company that I
>     bought it from is no longer in business, so I have no way of
>     getting passwords.  This is not that big of a concern, as I
>     am not going to be running any applications under VMS (I have
>     no idea where the site licenses are).

	>>> B/1

	SYSBOOT> SET UAFALTERNATE 1
	SYSBOOT> CONTINUE

		VAX/VMS ....   

	.....

	When the startup is done, log in as SYSTEM.  It will probably ask
you for TWO passwords, and you can type in anything at that point (UAFALTERNATE
sortof makes it not care).

	This should get you in.  From there, you will want to do:

		$ SHOW MEMORY

	to get an idea about the memory configuration.

> 8.) On the back of the unit, there are 3 switches by the main console
>     serial port.  The first is baud rate (figured that much out :)).
>     The second is a 3 position switch with fairly cryptic symbols,
>     a circle with a T in it, a sguigly line, one other I can't recall
>     off the top of my head.  What do they represent?  The third switch
>     is a toggle between a circle with a dot outside of it, and a circle
>     with a dot in the middle, what does this switch do?

	The second is a test switch.  The third is a "keyboard break enable"
switch.  If the dot is in the circle, a [BREAK] on the keyboard will put you
at the ">>>" console prompt.  If the dot is outside the circle, you have to
hit the "Halt" button (or "Reset") on the front panel to halt it.

> 9.) What is the unit worth on the street today?

	Depending upon the box ("suitcase" or "coffee-table") and other
configuration stuff, $1-2K?

> 10.) Is there any online documentation on some FTP site somewhere that
>      I should be looking at for some of this information?  I have spent
>      many hours searching, and have only found vague refferences so far.

	Look on your local technical book store for VMS-related books.  Also,
there is a "VMS System Managers Handbook" available from DEC for about $70.

