	In <29nff0$qe0@sol.ccs.deakin.edu.au> dougcc@brt.deakin.edu.au
	writes:

	>In Article <1993Oct14.120215.18465@dxcern.cern.ch>
	>lishka@dxcern.cern.ch (Christopher Lishka) writes:

	>>But my point still holds.  DEC claiming that VMS is "open"
	>>and then using the "multiple architecture" point is rather
	>>misleading.

	>Have DEC actually said this?  Can you give a reference?

In _Digital's Customer Update (Special Issue)_ from July 15, 1992, the
second article (starting on page 4) is titled "OpenVMS -- No
Compromise Computing with Open Standards."  The article first details
the renaming of VMS to OpenVMS.  Then it details "The Open Standards
of OpenVMS" with a list of features.  The list starts as follows:

	THE OPEN STANDARDS OF OpenVMS

	OpenVMS systems:

	* Now support multiple hardware architectures (VAX and Alpha
	  systems) and Alpha will be available from multiple vendors.

	* Have POSIX support (1003.1, 1003.2, 1003.4) for increased
	  portability of applications and users.

[Other features follow]


	>As you say such a point doesn't make a lot of sense, and is
	>inconsistent with DEC's long-standing position that
	>Open=Standards.

DEC lists "multiple hardware architectures (VAX and Alpha systems)" as
their first feature of OpenVMS being "open".  I think this is silly,
because the few hardware architectures that VMS currently supports are
all made by DEC, to my knowledge.  (In the future we may see VMS
running on third-party Alpha platforms....)


	>Only if you depend on the O/S.  The point of openness is
	>being able to use portable *applications* which you can run
	>on multiple O/S.

This is indeed true >if< the applications were designed to be
portable.

My personal opinion is the whole "open" hullabaloo is just a lot of
marketing BS.  As others have pointed out, VMS is about as "open" as
the commercial Unix systems.  If you are buying new computers you
evaluate what you need based on what you do.  You don't just buy a
large expensive computer because some marketing type has stuck a large
day-glo "OPEN" sticker on the packaging.
