In article <jonas.751639019@beor>, jonas@beor.informatik.rwth-aachen.de (Josef Nelissen) writes:

[ A system of the quoted configuration is running too slowly. ]


>We work on a DEC-system 5000/240, 64 MB, ULTRIX 4.3, INGRES 6.4/03.
>The ULTRIX system, the INGRES system and the database itself have
>their own discs, each of 1.3 GB, a fourth disc is used for PC-network.

Without more specific information it is hard to make specific
suggestions.  So, I'll make the general ones.

1.   Find the bottle neck.  What resource are you running out of?

     CPU speed?
     Disk I/O bandwidth (bytes/unit time)?
     Disk I/O through-put (requests/unit time)?
     Memory?

1a.  The commands iostat(1), vmstat(1) and cpustat(1) include the
     time spent in various CPU modes; user mode, nice, system and
     idle.  You can check these to see where the time is going.
     Typical systems run mostly in user and nice mode, with some
     system time and hopefully some idle time.

     A lack of idle time but "reasonable" balance between system
     and user modes, suggests the bottleneck is the CPU.  You can
     either get a faster CPU (see 260 upgrade) or see if the
     application could be more efficient.

1b.  The built-in and TURBOchannel SCSI adapters (PMAZ) available for the
     DECsystem 5000/240 are capable of moving about 5 MB/sec data each.
     You can use iostat(1) to see is being used.  Database applications
     that I've seen tend not to be bandwidth intensive.  The RZ58 disk
     you are probably use (1.3 GB) is capable of about 2 MB/sec reading
     at large transfer sizes.  Two of them transfering as fast as possible
     can saturate one bus.

1c.  From my experience databases tend to be more request intensive
     than bandwidth intensive.  The requests tend to be relatively
     small and only in extreme circumstances will they be limited by
     the bandwidth of either the disk or the controller.  What does
     limit them is that disks are mechanical and have limits of how
     many I/O requests they can complete in a given time.

     Again, iostat(1) will you give information about how busy the
     disks are.  The maximum request rate that can be supported is
     very dependent on the disk.  For rotating disks it is also
     dependent on how disk space the data is spread over.  I don't
     have an RZ58, but I do have some RZ57s.  The number below were
     obtained using a simple program.  I used different partitions
     to control the seek range.  The percentage given is the size
     of the partition used compared to the size of the device:

	   100.00%: 29.612480 tps.
	    31.44%: 33.398561 tps.
	     9.43%: 36.660399 tps.
	     1.68%: 40.680077 tps.

     The I/O size was 8 KB, since the transfer size will also have
     an affect.  These will give you a rough guess as to whether the
     disks are saturated.

1d.  Use vmstat(1) to look at the system paging stats.  The manual page
     is adequate for at least getting a clue as to whether paging is
     a problem.
 
2.  Once you identify the bottleneck(s), you can attempt to apply
    solutions to each.

a.  As noted before, if the CPU is saturated you either need a faster
    CPU or need to examine the application to see if it can be made
    more efficient.

b.  It is trivially easy these days to saturate a 5 MB/sec SCSI adapter.
    The only solution is to get additional adapters and spread the disks
    out.  The appliation is saturating a single disk, then you need a
    faster disk or Striping to get the bandwidth benefits of spreading
    the I/O across multiple disks.

c.  Similar to the bandwidth problem, if a disk is saturated you either
    need a faster disk or more disks to handle the I/O load.  Which is
    better depends on many things that I'll discuss in a later follow-up.

d.  Buy more memory.  There are some things you can do to make the system
    page and swap more quickly, but avoiding it in the first place is always
    best.  Using the 8 MB memory card for the Model 240 you can get as much
    as 120 MB in a system.  Using the 32 MB card you can get 480 MB.

3.  Other possible changes.

a.  Raw disk vs. the File system.  Most better DBMS allow using either
    raw devices or files on a file system.  When using a file on file
    system the DBMS has to allow for the presense of the UNIX buffer
    cache.  On writes it may prefer to do them all synchronously so that
    when a write claims to be complete it knows it really is.  Or it
    take advantage of the asynchronous nature of the writes and use
    journaling or some other technique to sure that no data is lost.

    When running on a raw device it may do its own buffering to avoid
    doing some I/Os (reads) and with journaling, some writes.  Which
    performs better depends on how well it does its own buffer management.
    It may be worth investigating the differences to see if changing is
    worth the effort.

b.  Prestoserve is a hardware/software layered product that includes a
    non-volatile memory board and the software to manage it.  It can
    be used with block devices or file systems to safely buffer
    synchronous writes.  In the right I/O loads it can make a significant
    difference in I/O performance.

c.  Included in the V4.3 documentation set is a performance guide.  It
    should offer other suggestions about how to study and improve general
    system performance.

