and made at least one typo in the commentary on 2b...

>    The appliation is saturating a single disk, then you need a
>    faster disk or Striping to get the bandwidth benefits of spreading
>    the I/O across multiple disks.

This should be preceded by an "If".

But more importantly promised...

>
>c.  Similar to the bandwidth problem, if a disk is saturated you either
>    need a faster disk or more disks to handle the I/O load.  Which is
>    better depends on many things that I'll discuss in a later follow-up.

If the DBMS insists on doing synchronous writes and has only one process
doing the writing there are few things you can do to make the writes
faster, except get a faster disk.  The problem in this case is that
the *next* write has to wait for the current to finish.  For this case
the best you can do is use a solid state disk.  Their seek time and
rotational latency are very small (having to neither seek nor rotate).
Unfortunately they tend to be more expensive than other disks.

In the single threaded I/O case Striping will do very little to help.
For a constant capacity device it may help some simply by having each
member be smaller and thus reducing the seek time.  When the I/O
is asynchronous or the I/O is multi-threaded Striping can help more
by spreading the I/O among multiple devices.  Spreading the I/O
among multiple controller can help even more as it allows multiple
transfers to take place at once.

This feature is somewhat limited when using the PMAZ because it
requires the CPU to copy data from the PMAZ intermediate buffer
to memory.  I have heard plans for support of the PMAZB (dual
channel, DMA SCSI adapter) on a future version of ULTRIX, so
this limitation may not be a problem eventually.  

