Actually not exactly.  Having pipe syntax in DCL is useful iff
there are facilities available that act nicely as tools.  This
means that commands must generate output that is appropriate for
use as the input of other commands.  To a large extent, VMS lacks
such commands.  To a large extent, such commands could be added,
by extending existing commands, and building new ones, and even
adopting appropriate commands from Unix or other systems.  There's
nothing especially tough about this, but it is more than just
adding pipes to DCL.

