In article <1993Oct13.180624.23099@dxcern.cern.ch>,
Dan Pop <danpop@cernapo.cern.ch> wrote:
>In <1993Oct13.170157.121316@embl-heidelberg.de> omond@embl-heidelberg.de (Roy Omond) writes:
>
>>Not only that, Carl, but in the case where it doesn't hit the "arg list too
>>long" bug, it causes each file to be "touched" which makes a nonsense of any
>>attempt to track usage of files.  Sheesh ... this whole thing is getting
>>verrrrry silly.
>
>Are you sure (about wc)?
>
[ listing showing last modified times unchanged by wc deleted. ]

I think what Roy meant was not that the files would be touched in the sense
of the utility "touch" (which usually changes last modified times), but that
the files' last accessed times would be changed.  Try the same example, but
use "ls -lu" instead of "ls -l", and you'll see the times change.

The *real* way to get the equivalent of the VMS functionality is to simply
do either "ls -l | head -1" or "ls -lR {dir} | head -1" to get the total
disk blocks used.  Unfortunately, this method only works when listing
directories (you can use the "-s" option instead of "-l" if you want.)
To get a grand total of file sizes without modifying last accessed times,
you need to do something like:

	"ls -s1 {files} | awk '{sum += $1;} END {print sum}'"

This is a bit clunky, but not too horrendous, especially if you hide it behind
an alias.

This operation should be easier to achieve on UNIX, and the fact that it isn't
points up a small hole in the coverage of the "ls" command.  However, the fact
that the missing feature can be compensated for so easily shows one of UNIX's
major strengths.  A similar feature "hole" on VMS will often require serious
programming to plug.

- Brad
