In article <1993Oct12.022947.1992@debug.cuc.ab.ca>, root@debug.cuc.ab.ca (Robert Franke) writes:
> 1.) If you had a MicroVax II, what would you do with it?  What O/S
>     would you run, etc?

  I'd upgrade it to a MicroVAX III 8-) Seriously, it depends on what you want
to do. I personally have 2 MicroVAX 3600's (these are MV III systems) and a
VAxstation 3100/38-SPX, along with a bunch of spare parts and MVII stuff. How-
ever, most of my development these days is on BSD/386, a Unix workalike for
PC hardware.

> 2.) What CPU is in a MicroVax II?  How fast is it?  How many users
>     can it handle at one time?

  The CPU is a KA630 CPU module, which uses the 78032 chip, which is a DEC-
specific part (like all microprocessor-based VAXes). It runs at .9 VUP (VAX
Unit of Processing), where a VAX 780 is 1 VUP. Depending on what you're try-
ing to do, you could probably get up to about 8 people banging away on it.
These were sold with varying licenses. I believe the maximum license group
was "40 users and up", so at one (optomistic) time, DEC must have assumed 
40 users was possible.

> 3.) What is the standard configuration of memory in a MicroVax II,
>     and how would I find out how much ram is in it?

  The CPU has either 256Kb or 1Mb on-board memory. You almost definitely have
1Mb on-board (most of the 256Kb modules were prototypes). The system can sup-
port up to 2 additional modules, which came in 1, 2, 4, and 8Mb capacity from
DEC (3rd-parties offered a 16Mb board as well). Since the CPU can only address
16Mb, the maximum is a CPU and 2 8Mb boards (or CPU and 1 16Mb board). The
CPU's on-board memory is disabled if there is 16Mb of external memory instal-
led.

  The VMS command "show memory" will show you the amount of configured memory.

> 4.) Other than "b" for boot, what options do I have at the pre-boot
>     prompt ">>>" thing?

 H(alt), a no-op, I(nit) and U(njam), one of which is a no-op and the other
generates a bus reset (I don't remember which, just that the classic "incan-
tation" to reboot a stuck VAX is H/I/U/B). C(ontinue), E(xamine), D(eposit),
and maybe T(est).

> 5.) I've heard rumours that there is a PD BSD Unix that runs on a
>     MicroVax II.  Is this true, and if it is, how would I get a
>     copy in TK50 tape format that would boot and load the harddrive?

  There is no such thing as a PD Unix (yet). There are freely-redistributable
releases based on the CSRG "Net-2" release, which is a post-4.3BSD with all
of the USL copyrighted material removed. That isn't enough to create a work-
ing system without writing a *lot* of code.

  Assuming you had a USL source license, you could get one of the 4.3BSD or
later releases. Unfortunately, the original 4.3BSD support for the MVII wasn't
complete, and later releases (Tahoe, Reno, etc.) became less and less stable,
at least on VAX hardware.

> 6.) How much does Ultrix for the MV II cost?  Is it even availlable?

  More than you could possibly imagine (in other words, well over $1000). If
you are interested, call DEC at 800-DIGITAL, ask for pre-sales support, and
get the part number for it.

> 7.) Currently the system is running VMS, but ofcourse, I have no
>     OPERATOR or SYSTEM passwords.  Is there an over-ride ability
>     that can be done from the main console? The company that I
>     bought it from is no longer in business, so I have no way of
>     getting passwords.  This is not that big of a concern, as I
>     am not going to be running any applications under VMS (I have
>     no idea where the site licenses are).

  Yes. This is described in various VMS manuals (which you don't have), de-
pending on what release of VMS it is. I'll send you a copy of this with the
instructions (when someone posts them, they always get flamed, despite the
fact that they're in the manuals, well-known by crackers, and the bad guys
could always just walk out with the MVII anyway).

> 8.) On the back of the unit, there are 3 switches by the main console
>     serial port.  The first is baud rate (figured that much out :)).
>     The second is a 3 position switch with fairly cryptic symbols,
>     a circle with a T in it, a sguigly line, one other I can't recall
>     off the top of my head.  What do they represent?  The third switch
>     is a toggle between a circle with a dot outside of it, and a circle
>     with a dot in the middle, what does this switch do?

  Ah, yes. One of DEC's first ventures into "internationalization". The
switch with no comprehensible symbols. The circle-T is self-test loop. The
picture of a face is "ask what language to use on the console". The other
position is "normal operation". The dot-and-circle switch is the autoboot
switch. It selects whether the system boots the OS on power-up or sits at
the ">>>" prompt.

> 9.) What is the unit worth on the street today?

  Unless you have some particularly rare options in it, not much. I've seen
MVII's w/ 8Mb, 1 RD54 disk, and TK tape going for a few hundred $ in casual
sales. Of course, if you went to buy one from a dealer, you'd pay a lot more.
However, if you asked them to make an offer for yours, they'd laugh.

> 10.) Is there any online documentation on some FTP site somewhere that
>      I should be looking at for some of this information?  I have spent
>      many hours searching, and have only found vague refferences so far.

  There isn't a great deal of MVII-specific info around. You can buy the man-
uals from DEC, but on a hobby system you probably don't need them - just ask
on the net when you need to know something.

