In article <sgj2gRW00WBM04yMIh@andrew.cmu.edu>, sh4j+@andrew.cmu.edu (Samuel Roy Helwig) writes:
> So, I was wondering if it is possible to adapt the TK50
> drive into another system.  Does it use a standard interface or some
> DEC-special interface?  Anyone have any idea how to get the drive into
> something other than the Vaxstation (if it is even possible...)?

  The TK50 uses a dual serial data bus to talk to the controller. One of the
serial loops is the command/status path, while the other (faster) loop is the
data path. You can buy a board from DEC to turn the drive into a pseudo-SCSI
drive. However, the board costs more than a new QIC/DAT/etc. drive, so I don't
know anyone who has bothered to do this.

  DEC has a very nice SCSI re-implementation of the TK50 (well, actually it's
built for them by a vendor in Japan) called the TZ30. It's a 1/2-height drive
and works quite well in DEC systems. However, it doesn't report some info via
Inquiry and Mode Sense that other systems want to see, so even it won't work
on your garden-variety Unix box without some tweaking.

