>Maybe you don't need a copy of OS for each machine, but this is not
>what
>constitutes the strength of VMScluster. Can you set your Unix
>Workstations 
>so, that a crash of any sigle node doesn't affect the users, except
>those
>logged on directly to the crashed node? Or such, that a user can send
>a
>program for background execution, and the program will execte on the
>least busy machine available?

Depends on the environment. In a workstation environment, failover is
non-existant. I don't know about batch load balancing, but I think NQS
(freely available) might handle it. VMSclusters are industry leading
technology for large shops, but for workstations, don't have a very big
edge over unix systems. So if we're talking desktop, there isn't much
difference. If we're talking datacenter/server stuff, VMS has a serious
edge that the unix community is working on catching up to right now
(c.f. SparcClusters). IBM is also lagging behind in this area on its
mainframe systems. Kudos to DEC on this one. Again, just goes to show
you where VMS' future lies.
