In article <29enij$ivr@hpfcbig.sde.hp.com>, marc@sde.hp.com (Marc Sabatella) writes:
="Parsing" was the wrong for me to have used; the original post mentioned
="specification of file arguments".  That is, for a given command that operates
=on a set of files, how is that set of files specified?  The Unix model is to
=allow a list to be presented on stdin, so you can generate the list however you
=want - including by listing them in a text file.  The original implication was
=that VMS programs included code to handle various methods of specification.  I
=have since been informed that the poster was incorrect - VMS programs do not
=include such code, but rather, the "shell" handles it all.  This then
=eliminates the inconsistency problem, but it does nothing to eliminate the
=customization problem ("but I *want* to specify my files this way that the
=shell doesn't support").

Your above paragraph is incomprehensible.  First of all, the unix model for
specifying files is to have the shell deal with any wild-cards and hand the
program the resulting list of files as arguments.  VMS does NOT have a "shell,"
and even if you insist on calling DCL a shell, it STILL doesn't "handle it all"
in the sense you seem to be claiming.  Any image that wants to handle
wild-carded filespecs is responsible for generating its own list of filespecs
from the wild-carded versions.  However, the library routine LIB$FIND_FILE will
take care of figuring out what the syntax of the filespec means.  It's not at
all difficult to write a subroutine to call LIB$FIND_FILE and generate a list
of filenames in the standard UNIX style.  If you want to muck around with
non-standard syntax for filespecs, you need only have the subroutine do its own
preparsing on the filespec it's supplied before handing it to LIB$FIND_FILE.
Here's a simple subroutine that handles standard VMS filespecs (including, if
you wish, remote node specifications and access control strings):

/******************************************************************************\
 * get_files:  Expand a wild-carded filespec, placing the results into an     *
 *	array of character pointers;                                          *
 * Arguments:                                                                 *
 *  char *spec : input filespec;                                              *
 *  int *nfiles: pointer to integer to receive count of matching filespecs    *
 *  char ***filelist : pointer to character pointer pointer to receive address*
 *	of array of character pointers to resulting files                     *
\******************************************************************************/

#include descrip
#define $DESCRIPTOR_D(name)    struct dsc$descriptor_d name = \
    { 0, DSC$K_DTYPE_T, DSC$K_CLASS_D, 0 }

get_files(char *spec, int *nfiles, char ***filelist)
{   $DESCRIPTOR(filespec, spec);
    $DESCRIPTOR_D(result);
    int context, i;
    struct NODE
    {   struct NODE *NODE_nxtnode;
        char *NODE_value;
    } *root_node, *node;

    root_node = (struct NODE *) malloc(sizeof(struct NODE));
    node = root_node;
    context = *nfiles = 0;
    filespec.dsc$w_length = strlen(spec);
    while((LIB$FIND_FILE(&filespec, &result, &context, 0, 0, 0, 0) & 7) == 1)
    {   node->NODE_nxtnode = (struct NODE *) malloc(sizeof(struct NODE));
        node->NODE_value = (char *) malloc(result.dsc$w_length + 1);
        strncpy(node->NODE_value, result.dsc$a_pointer, result.dsc$w_length);
        node->NODE_value[result.dsc$w_length] = '\0';
        ++*nfiles;
        node = node->NODE_nxtnode;
    }
    LIB$FIND_FILE_END(&context);
    *filelist = (char **) malloc((*nfiles + 1) * sizeof(char *));
    for(i = 0, node = root_node; i < *  nfiles; ++i)
    {   (*filelist)[i] = node->NODE_value;
        node = node->NODE_nxtnode;
        free(root_node);
        root_node = node;
    }
    (*filelist)[i] = 0;
}
/******************************************************************************/

Here's an example of using it to print out a list of all filespecs matching
each f the arguments passed to a program:

main(int nargs, char **args)
{   char **files;
    int nfiles, i, j;
    for(j = 1; j < nargs; ++j)
    {   get_files(args[j], &nfiles, &files);
        for(i = 0; i < nfiles; ++i)
            puts(files[i]);
    }
}
