In article <1993Oct14.113900.6710@dxcern.cern.ch>, lishka@dxcern.cern.ch (Christopher Lishka) writes:
=For which I apologize.  I used the SET FILE example because I >had<
=learned about directories-as-directories and directories-as-files, so
=I always to file-level operations on the *.DIR with (SET FILE).

Which can be a big mistake.  For example, the commands:
	$ SET FILE/VERSION_LIMIT=1 FUBAR.DIR;1
and
	$ SET DIRECTORY/VERSION_LIMIT=1 [.FUBAR]
are NOT equivalent.  In general, under VMS, if you want to treat a directory as
a file, you specify it as a file.  If you want to treat it as a directory, you
treat it as a directory.  There are, of course, some cases in which you don't
have the option (e.g., SET ACL;  it would be nice if DEC were to add a
/OBJECT=DIRECTORY qualifier to SET ACL.  I.e., I think it would be nice
of:
	$ SET ACL/OBJ=FILE/ACL=(ID=user,OPT=DEFAULT,ACCESS=R+W+E) fubar.dir;1
and
	$ SET ACL/OBJ=DIRECTORY/ACL=(ID=user,ACCESS=R+W+E) [.FUBAR]
were synonyms for each other).
