In article <CExyrx.23G@info.bris.ac.uk>, tjl@siva.bristol.ac.uk (Tim
Llewellyn) writes:
>yes pipes would be nice wouldn't they. And $DIRECTORY/PAGE would also
>be
>very useful. Of course one can always $ directory/out=foo.txt and then
>$ type/page foo.txt, or even write a .COM file to do it with a single
>command line. The lack of pipes in VMS means sometimes it requires
>more
>commands to do things that for systems with pipes. But its still easy 
>enough to get them done (assuming one knows what one is doing).

This is a DCL issue, not VMS. DCL just makes such things inconvenient.
Either you do it in 3 commands (don't forget to delete your temp file),
or you write a .com file, if you think you'll ever use it again, and can
keep track of a vast number of such itty bitty .com files that do such
tiny tasks. But if VMS had pipes, you could do this ad hoc. 

If you did it a lot, rather than writing a com file, putting it
somewhere and defining a symbol to run it, you could make it a symbol
(alias on unix) if DCL would do pipes, or even multiple commands per
line with a substitution facility for symbols (I'm dreaming, but it
wouldn't be to tough to do). VMS doesn't have pipes, but it does have
mailboxes, which are sufficient for this sort of thing, if DCL took the
trouble to use them.

This seems like such a tiny example, some (like DEC) might say, "so
what, it isn't worth a major amount of work to enable such tiny things".
However, such tiny things come up very often. Assuming you know both of
them equally well, it's just easier to do most sorts of ad hoc things
like this on unix. By ad hoc, I mean simple tasks that aren't quite
addressed by standard utilities, but can be hacked together fairly
easily. (Which is why I was so looking forward to VMS/POSIX, till I
actually got it.) This raises the frustration factor of VMS/DCL a long
way.

>btw which OS would you rather deal with with a hangover?

Neither, get real! But if I had to do something with which I was
familiar, unix. For me, it has a lower frustration factor. If not,
whichever one had the best and most easily scanned documentation. In my
personal experience, that's VMS, but the only unix I've used in 6.5
years is Intergraph, and their on-line docs, other than xman, aren't
accessible from X, which is the only windowing environment available to
me.
