In article <1993Oct14.140420.2280@ivax>, imhw400@indyvax.iupui.edu (Mark
H. Wood) writes:
>If I want to SET something or DELETE something, what else would I use?
>
Well, OSes I have used have used the following verbs, or abbreviations
thereof in the case of unix, to get rid of a file on disk: eliminate,
remove, erase, expunge, purge, and delete. If you didn't already know
the right one, none of these seems more likely than another. The average
person would ask the knowlegeable user "how do I get rid of this file?".
Yet I've never seen one that'd handle "GET RID OF filename". :-) 

While set is the most obvious verb for many of the things it does in
DCL, MODIFY, ALTER, or CHANGE would be equally or more obvious for many
of them, and in many cases a more specific verb would be more obvious,
but that would, of course, lead to many more verbs to learn and
remember. The best example may be SET MAGTAPE, where most of the
qualifiers don't _set_ anything, they _do_ things, like rewind, skip
forward or backwards, unload the tape, etc. Unix has an mt "verb" for
this, which is clearer. The few actual set options could be moved to SET
DEVICE.

Is the fact that DCL uses rename to move a file from one directory to
another any clearer than the fact that unix uses mv (move) to rename a
file within the same directory? (I've had novices who understand rename
ask me how to move a file, it isn't obvious if you don't know about it.)
And of course, the verb to move a file to another device on VMS is
BACKUP/DELETE (COPY/DELETE doesn't exist), whereas unix uses mv (move),
which is definitely clearer.

Now, if I delete a queue with DELETE/QUEUE, a symbol with DELETE/SYMBOL,
and a batch job with DELETE/ENTRY (there's an obvious one), then I
delete a file with DELETE/FILE, right? Sorry. The command set started
out with more consistency than it has today. 

On a SET command, you have all the following forms:

SET option value
SET option/qual value
SET [NO]option              (the NO is optional)
SET option=value
SET option/qual=value

Only the first 2 make any sense relative to each other. A weak case can
be made for the third, but wouldn't SET option {ON|OFF} be more
obvious?

On other commands, it's very often not at all obvious whether something
will be a parameter or a qualifier. SET VOLUME requires the volume name
(if you are changing it) as a qualifier value (the parameter is the
device containing the volume), whereas MOUNT requires it as a
parameter.

DCL commands are getting longer as time goes on. Compare 

SET SECURITY/CLASS=DEVICE/PROTECTION=(...) MUA0:

to

SET PROTECTION=(...)/DEVICE MUA0:

(This is a typical example of a definite trend.) It is now possible
(easy, even) to overrun DCL's tiny input buffer with a perfectly
reasonable command. CREATE/TERMINAL with lots of options comes
immediately to mind, as well as some of the things you wish you could do
in a makefile, and I know it's happened in other cases as well.

Ironically, the system with longer commands has smaller buffers to hold
them. Unix, becuase the shell expands wildcards, and because of the
backquote mechanism, tends to support command buffers of 10K and up.
DCL's input buffer is 256 bytes, with the internally expanded command
limited to 1K. (Hope those are right, if not I'll get flamed, but they
are close.)

Sorry, DCL is _not_ a perfect command language. DCL's commands are
easier to remember once you know them, because they are almost all
english words, and most are to some degree mnemonic, at least once you
know the logic behind them. unix commands, on the other hand, are way
easier to type. As an expert user, I'd prefer unix. As a novice, I'd
prefer DCL. But on neither system can a novice intuit the correct
command to do anything at all.

(Thanks to the DCL HELP facility, which provided most of the ammunition
for this article, and is in many ways vastly superior to its typical
unix counterparts.)
