Reply sent to the newsgroup and via mail.  

>I've currently got a VAXserver 3300 disk serving my little cluster of diskless
>3100s and uVAX IIs.  I want to share the user disk with my unix cluster, making
>a common home disk.  This is possible through Multinet NFS, but i have some
>serious problems.  Due to the stress that NFS serving puts on the 3300, I
>often see unreasonable time lags between disk requests from the NFS clients
>and the response from the server.  Some applications won't work at all, claiming
>that the NFS partition doesn't exist.  
>
>I'm currently using the standard ethernet controller on the 3300 (inherent to
>the motherboard), and my first question is what is this board's rated
>throughput, and would that be a source of my lag problems.  

Could be you're attempting to send too much data across the Ethernet, which 
means that no matter how fast your Ethernet controller is, you're not going 
to get more pushed through your 10Mb/sec (theortical) pipe.

so:  - how much data are you trying to send? (hookup a line analyzer)
     - do you have excessive (unnecessary) traffic going between your NI-
       cluster nodes (like locking traffic?)?
     - how busy is the NFS-served disk (MONITOR DISK/ITEM=QUEUE)?
     - how busy is the 3300 itself (MONITOR SYSTEM)?
     - have you run AUTOGEN on the system recently (with feedback)?
     - have you contacted TGV to ensure your NFS server is running as 
       well as it can?
     - are you sure the problem is at the 3300 and not slow links or 
       slow bridges on your network?  How busy is your network?

>I RTFM'd the
>manual, but it says nowhere what this board is capable of, just that it exists,
>and that an additional board may be installed which leads to my next question: 
>If I put another controller in there, (a DELQA, when I find one), can it
>be configured so that one card handles DECNet traffic to the VMS clients
>and the other handles TCP/IP traffic to the world (including the unix nfs 
>clients).

In a VMScluster, DECnet is not used for the file transfers, locking, or 
other cluster communications between nodes.

