It was probably a relabled PDT-11/130.  The PDT (Programmable Data
Terminal) line of machines included the 11/110 (CPU in a VT100 case),
11/130 (CPU + 2 TU58's in a VT100 case) and the 11/150 (a separate
CPU with two 8" floppies).  It was based on the LSI-11 (11/2, 11/03)
chip set and had 60kb of on-board memory.  I'm not sure about the
11/110 (never saw one), but the 11/130 and 11/150 could have 4
serial lines, one comm (sync/async) line and a printer port.

I think the only Digital operating system which supported it was
RT-11, but you might have to get an older version in order to have
it run.  RT stopped shipping a PDT-bootable disk/tape a few releases
before V5.6 (the latest).

					Megan Gentry
