In article <1993Oct13.100910.28656@dxcern.cern.ch>, 
    					danpop@cernapo.cern.ch (Dan Pop) writes:
> I've seen a lot of questions of the type: "What's the Unix equivalent of
> this VMS command?" Now, I have a practical question the other way round.
> 
> My boss has a Unix account and a VMS account. On his Unix account he has
> a .forward file like this:
> 
> boss@domain1
> boss@domain2
> "|/bin/cat >>/usr/mail/boss ; echo >>/usr/mail/boss"
> 
> For the VMS only people, this file forwards all the incoming email to two
> different addresses and also keeps a local copy, in the "newmail" folder
> (VMS terminology).
> 
> Now, he asked me how to do the same thing for his VMS account. Being
> rather ignorant in VMS, my idea was to look at SET FORWARD, but
> apparently this allows forwarding to only one address and no possibility
> to keep a local copy.
> 
> Is there any possibility to emulate the functionality of this .forward
> file in VMS, with plain user privileges?

        Yes.  Pick up the PD version  of DELIVER, available on the DECUS
    tapes,  file  servers and ftp sites everywhere :-).  If any  of  the
    CERN VAXen are  running  PMDF,  DELIVER  is  included  in  the  PMDF
    distribution.   Another  possibility  is  to  use  MX,  which  is  a
    replacement for the VMS Mail  interface (mail user agent?).  Neither
    require  privileges to _use_, but MX may require the system  manager
    to INSTALL the image and/or define some logical names in the  SYSTEM
    logical name table.

        I'll note here that when you use either of these packages to set
    up forwarding to a list of recipients, be sure to specify your boss'
    VMS account with a leading underscore to prevent looping:

        	boss@domain1
    		boss@domain2
    		_boss		! Save copy on local node

        You can get both DELIVER and  MX from Andy Harper's file server.
    Send  e-mail to VMSSERV@BAY.CC.KCL.AC.UK with the following lines in
    the body of the message:

	SEND DELIVER.PACKAGE
	SEND MX.PACKAGE

        Both  packages  are  also  available   via  anonymous  FTP  from
    FTP.SPC.EDU  in the [.MACRO32] subdirectory, (there is also a mirror
    site in Europe, but I don't have its name at  hand...)  or  via  the
    file  server  FILESERV@WKUVX1.BITNET  with similar commands to those
    given above.

        For non-VMS experts:  the  file  servers  return the packages in
    multi-part  VMS_SHARE format.  Extract each of the parts from  mail,
    concatenate them into a single file _IN_ORDER_, and  strip  off  any
    mail  header lines from the beginning of the concatenated file.  The
    result is a DCL command file which you simply execute:

    	MAIL> read 1
    	MAIL> extr/nohead MX.01
    	MAIL> read
    	MAIL> extr/nohead MX.02
                   .
    		   .
    	MAIL> extr/nohead MX.19

    	$ edit mx.01			! Get rid of mail headers and save.
    	$ append/new mx.%% mx.com
    	$ @mx

    If this seems tedious,  I'll  just  remark  that  there are packages
    which  do  all this for you, but the first time, ya got ta do it  by
    hand...
    	
            -Ken
