OK: I was a little hasty.  My reading of your statement (which seemed
to be promoting a feature of VMS) was that you implied that all or
most programs could be moved from 1981's VAXen to todays AXPs without
problems.  My counter example shows that this is not necessarily true.

For that matter, I am sure that somebody can dredge up an old program
that ran under some old version of Unix which now runs on a new
version of Unix without recompilation (given the same hardware).  To
me this is not necessarily a useful thing, though: if you can't
recompile on the new hardware and get it to work, then the forward
compatibility is useless.  What happens when your 1981 program breaks
on the next OS version (for example, because major portions of the OS
have been restructured as in VMS/VAX 6.0), and all of a sudden you
find you can't recompile either?

