On 11 Oct 93 12:10:43 CET, Tom Wade VMS Systems <T.Wade@vms.eurokom.ie> said:
Wade> To me, this is one of the biggest deficiencies in the Unix
Wade> shell: you can't abbreviate commands or options.  Therefore the
Wade> commands and more importantly the many options must be short to
Wade> avoid long typing.  Even worse, the getopt function requires a
Wade> single character argument (note: I learned this in
Wade> correspondence from a Unix guy - I have no experience with this)
Wade> which gives rise to commands like ZIP with about 24 options, all
Wade> single-characters with -V totally different to -v.

Nitpicky point: no abbreviation of options is a limitation of the
individual programs, not the shell--the shell just passes all the
options onto the program.

Substantive point: GNU getopt accepts long options (preceeded by '--')
and lets you abbreviate as long as the abbreviation is unambiguous.
For example, 'gzip --he' lists the options

usage: gzip [-cdfhlLnrtvV19] [-S suffix] [file ...]
 -c --stdout      write on standard output, keep original files unchanged
 -d --decompress  decompress
 -f --force       force overwrite of output file and compress links
 -h --help        give this help
 -l --list        list .gz file contents
 -L --license     display software license
 -n --no-name     do not save or restore the original name
 -q --quiet       suppress all warnings
 -r --recurse     recurse through directories
 -S .suf  --suffix .suf     use suffix .suf instead of .gz
 -t --test        test compressed file integrity
 -v --verbose     verbose mode
 -V --version     display version number
 -1 --fast        compress faster
 -9 --best        compress better
 file...          files to (de)compress. If none given, use standard input.

there are other UNIX argument parsers that allow long options, but GNU
getopt seems to be the most widely used.

Finally, a plea: if you want to continue this thread, *please* respect
the Follow-To: comp.unix.user-friendly, where this has just recently
been discussed to death.

