In article <tpw-111093143604@mac143.civil.nwu.edu>, tpw@nwu.edu (Tim Ward) writes:
>
>I am a total newbie with respect to DEC and it's equipment, but somebody
>decided to give us some MicroVaxes (one uVax 2 and one uVax 3+) and an
>assortment of 3200s (7 DECStations?) so I need to find out some info.  
>
>Around here most folks are using DOS on the desktop and UNIX on their
>bigger boxes for number crunching and other things.  If the donation
>doesn't fall through, I would like them to run UNIX, not VMS as they are
>currently configured.  I was figuring on setting them up to use the basic
>Xtools and some other GNU stuff.  Is converting these things to Ultrix
>possible?  Is it relatively straightforward?

Yes.  Buy the current VAX ULTRIX kit and nine licenses.  If you're going
to put all on a common (Ethernet) network and once has a some spare disk
space you can turn it into a RIS server for doing the installations of
the rest.  This is usually much faster than tape.  The first one you'll
have to install by tape (TK50).  Hopefully the VAXstation 3200s will have
at least an RD54 for the system disk (170+ MB).  If not you'll have a
hard time fitting a usable ULTRIX onto anything smaller.  At that point
hope that one has a more spare disk space so you can turn it into a
DMS server and run the clients mostly diskless.

In recent history the only "3200" of anything we've released is the
VAXstation 3200.  It used a ~3 VUP CPU and sat in a pedistal 
enclosure.  Typically it had one RD54 and an TK50.  The MicroVAX III
was the packaging for the same base CPU as in the VAXstation 3200.
One version of the package used the BA123 World Box.  The other used
a cabinet about the size of an 3-high RA81 cabinet, but a little
wider.  This probably had two RA disks, top and bottom and the
CPU in the middle.  Using RA82s it would have about 1 GB of disk
space.

