In article <29didbINNcli@gap.caltech.edu>, carl@SOL1.GPS.CALTECH.EDU (Carl J Lydick) writes:
|> How about if you want the total space taken up by all the files with names of
|> the form *.com?

   wc *.com

This gives the number of lines, words, and characters in all files of the form
"*.com" with a total at the end.  If you really object to being told the number
of lines and words, you can use "wc -c *.com" to only count characters.

Finally, if you really only want the total, you can get this with:

   wc -l *.com | tail -1

Hugh McIntyre.
Flash Memory IC Design.
INMOS Ltd., Bristol, UK.

BTW: I know about rounding errors caused by files only using part of the final
     block, which this doesn't tell you about.  But it does give you extra info
     such as the number of lines which may be useful if you want to know how
     many pages of printout would be needed to print them out (for example).

     Oh, and I do agree it would be better for du to have a "-t" option to give
     you a total.


