In article <CELA25.CHt@ses11.shpk.ses.com>, 
david@sesinc.com (SES, Inc) writes:

> DOes anyone know of  a vendor who has a SCSI II controller for a Q bus??  
> Specifically, I have a client that needs an interface to a SCSI device on a 
> PDP11 running RSX11M+.

The one I use (and like very much) is the SCDC from Andromeda Systems, Inc.
Everything can be configured either from a terminal plugged into
the controller board or via the system console (using a "boot" ROM
on the board).   It supports both disk (DU:) and tape (MU:) devices
and has an on-board cache (this makes a CDROM really fly!).   In
addition to the usual seven SCSI devices, it also supports up to
four floppy disks.   Floppy disk formatting is supported in all 
the usual DEC formats.

You can also partition each physical disk drive into multiple
logical units.   For instance, you could split a 1.0 GB drive into
four 250 MB logical units (DU0: through DU3:).

Another nice feature is the ability to update the microcode via
a modem for free.   This requires an optional plug and switch
panel to allow you to properly switch the connections between
the board, terminal, and modem.   It is well worth the small extra 
charge.

By the way, the limit in RSX-11M (and M-Plus) SYSGEN on only four
DU units on one controller is artificial.   If DEC has not already
done it, just edit the command file so the limit is something like
16.

