The DECserver 100 is quite outdated, and does not support TCP/IP.  It 
sounds like you're looking at the used market, so I'd urge you, if at all 
possible, to get a used DECserver 300 or possibly a new DECserver 700 from 
a 3rd party source if you're not needing modem control -- sometime in the 
future you may wish you had a DECserver that can do TCP/IP and is still 
having bugs corrected by Digital.

>We now have our modems on DZ11s, using only pins 2 & 3. DTR is forced on in
>all our modems; they disconnect on loss of carrier. No problems.
>
>I'm pretty sure I can use the 100's... but will it be faster if I have the
>modem control?

No, I wouldn't expect things to be faster; however, without modem control, 
I don't believe the DECserver will have any way of detecting that the 
remote modem has disconnected; thus, you someone could call that phone 
number again and re-connect to someone else's session.

How are your modems currently informing the VAX that the carrier was lost 
if only pins 2 and 3 are connected to the DZ11?

The DECserver 200 comes in two flavors, if I remember:  a "DECserver 200"
and a "DECserver 200/MC" - MC = modem control.  The DECserver 700 comes in 
several configurations, including 8- and 16-port models without modem 
control and an 8-port model with modem control.

