In article <1993Oct8.203318.18135@vpnet.chi.il.us>, kferg@vpnet.chi.il.us (Kevin Ferguson) writes:
> In article <28pio0$sgh@jac.nuo.dec.com>,
> Steve Lionel <lionel@quark.enet.dec.com> wrote:
> 
>    [...snip...]
>>
>>If you're dealing with converting floating-point data between a
>>big-endian IEEE system and VAX, you'll need to swap the byte order of
>>integers and do a big-endian-IEEE-to-VAX floating conversion.  
> 
>    [...snip...]
> 
> I have a question regarding the IEEE-to-VAX F_floating point conversion.
> 
> I was recently presented with a pair of routines that
> supposedly performed this conversion in each direction.  The
> amazing thing was (to me anyway) that they obtained the VAX
> float format by swapping bytes as you indicated and then
> just multiplying by 4.  <jaw hits floor>
> 
> I was under the inpression that, due to the different 
> normalized forms of both the exponent and mantissa, the 
> conversion was more involved than that.
> 
> If memory serves me right (I don't have a copy of IEEE-754),
> IEEE format uses an excess-127 form for its exponent and represents
> the most-significant fraction bit.  The VAX F_floating point uses
> an excess-128 form and the most-significant fraction bit is
> implicitly assumed.
> 
> Am I missing something here?

        Nope, you've got  it  right.   Actually  it's  quite  easy to see.
    After  swapping the bytes (well words) of a VAX F_float, it looks very
    much like an IEEE single: sign-bit in the left-most position, followed
    by 8 bits of exponent, followed by 23 bits of mantissa.

        IEEE chose to use the 8-bits of mantissa differently from the VAX:
    they chose to  use  an  approximately  symmetric  range  of minimum to
    maximum  values (1.7*10**-38 to 1.7*10**38 as compared to 0.29*10**-38
    to 1.7*10*38), and they can do de-normalized numbers, NaN's and Inf's,
    etc., with the bit patterns that are "left over" compared to  the  VAX
    format.

        The two important  points  are:  1)  if  you  take  the 8-bit IEEE
    exponent and interpret it as excess-127 instead of excess-128, you get
    one factor of two; and 2) if you take the normalization of IEEE:

		1.bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb

    and compare to the VAX normalization:

		0.1bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb

    you get a second factor of two.   Result: a simple factor of 4 between
    the  two  representations...at least for normalized values within  the
    common range of both representations.

        BTW, I'll bet is  faster  to  simply  add  or  subtract 2 from the
    exponent field than it is to do a floating multiply-by-4, although you
    do have to do bit-field manipulations...

	-Ken
