David L. Cathey <davidc@alpha.montagar.com> wrote:
>In article <CEHsC8.57y@biles.com>, daniels@biles.com (Brad Daniels) writes:
...
>> Actually, it's the same on all of those systems.  What is the equivalent
>> of DIR/SEC on HP VMS....  Oh, you mean VMS isn't available on HP?
>
>	No, /Security isn't available on HP!

Actually, HP/UX happens to support a pretty good set of security features,
including ACL's.  Of course, security isn't one of the "standard" parts
of HP/UX, but U*IX doesn't preclude security.

...
>> Now, I'm not claiming UNIX options aren't cryptic.  They usually are.
>> There are also two flavors of system utilities (Sys V-derived and BSD-
>> derived) which can create pain when you go from one type of system
>> to another until you learn both.
>
>	Standard Unix?

There's standard System V UNIX, and standard BSD.  They're actually remarkably
compatible, since there's a lot of cross-pollination.  This is part of the
nature of standards.  There will always be multiple competing standards,
whether official or simply de facto, in any given problem space.  In general,
though, when multiple standards are sufficiently similar that a merge is
possible, the merge tends to happen, one way or another (witness e.g. OSF's
"ps" accepting both the Sys V and BSD sets of options).  VMS has the advantage
of being specified, implemented, and controlled by a single entity.  There's
little room for version skew in such a situation, and no need for merging
disparate versions.

>> VMS is great when you're doing the things it helps you do.  It just doesn't
>> help you do as many different things as *NIX does.  The problem with *NIX
>> is that it takes a lot of work to learn what it really does for you.
>
>	This is why I think that Unix is never going to be a pwerful
>force on the desktop.  Users don't want to take a lot to learn what it does.
>They just want it to do what they want it to.  That's why DOS/Windows have
>done so well, and not SCO Unix (not to say that they are doing lousy).

Have you looked at HP VUE?  As a U*IX "expert" managing multiple different
platforms, I personally find it a pain, since it takes over so much of the
configuration, but the environment allows a novice user to ignore system
and command line issues far more effectively than even something like Windows
does.  Windows NT is getting really close to what I'd view as a viable
replacement for UNIX (and VMS) as an operating system, but it has a ways
to go...  The command line interface is inadequate and the graphical interface
is not yet sufficiently complete or flexible.  Besides, we've been comparing
command line interfaces up to now...  Don't tell me you view VMS as a viable
alternative to Windows?

>>                                             I just find *NIX much more
>> flexible and better suited to my style and purposes.
>
>	And obviously others find VMS better suited to their style and
>purpose...

Yeah, but they obviously haven't given UNIX a *real* chance. :-) Casual
users will rarely be thrilled by the UNIX command line.  Things like the
ability to casually start and stop processes without worrying about startup
times and system loads, reading and writing files without having to worry
about carriage control and record formatting getting in the way, etc. are
the things that usually start winning people over when they come from VMS.
It takes a bit of time and effort to learn to appreciate the capabilities
of the shells and utilities, but once you learn them you miss them terribly
in any environment which doesn't have equivalents.

- Brad
