In article <1993Oct8.145959.17029@vax.oxford.ac.uk> nagist@vax.oxford.ac.uk (NAG Software Engineering Group) writes:
>In an Army Surplus store in Oxford (England) today I saw a rack of 4 DEC RA60
>drawer-type disk drives in the window. They've got a sign on the rack, it
>says: "Offers invited" -- any idea what they might be worth? (nothing?).
>
>Can Anybody tell me what their spec is? how old they are? Are they PDP-11 or
>VAX vintage?

Well, they're both.  They ran off both UDA50A controllers on PDP11's or Vax
Unibus and HSC's.

They were an interesting design -- they were designed to give an inexpensive
removable storage device for customers (like the military) that wanted to pull
their secure data and stick in a vault like they did with the RP06/RM05/RM03 
disks that they were used to, but still use the newer technology (DSA) drives.

Since RA81's weren't too porable the RA60 was born.

Bill
