	In <CELCq8.94B@info.bris.ac.uk> tjl@siva.bristol.ac.uk (Tim
	Llewellyn) writes:

[Regarding Unix "man" vs. VMS "HELP":]

	>aw come-on, you can't seriously believe the hideously
	>horrible man is better than the nice nested VMS HELP...?

I agree with this: VMS' HELP on the whole is nicer than Unix's "man".
However, that does not mean that VMS' HELP cannot be improved, or that
Unix's manual pages don't have some nice features that VMS' HELP lacks.

One aspect where Unix's "man" shines over VMS' HELP is that Unix manual
pages contain cross-references to other manual pages.  So, if the
information does not appear in the manual page you are currently
reading, then you can look at the other manual pages referenced.
Having spent a lot of time looking for ways to do things in both VMS
and Unix, I find it >much< easier in Unix because the manual page
cross-references tell me other places that have related information.
If you don't find what you want in a VMS HELP entry, you almost have
to free associate to think up other places to search in.

Plus, Unix has "man -k <word>" which will report manual pages that
mention <word> in the summary.  e.g.  "man -k dir" lists a lot of
commands that likely have something to do with directories.  I wish
VMS HELP had this feature....

On the whole, though, VMS' HELP is a lot easier to use than Unix's
manual pages.
