In Article <CEHIoL.201@fc.hp.com> chan@fc.hp.com (Channing Benson) writes:

>The real joke is "Open VMS". Open to what exactly?

OpenVMS conforms to a wide range of widely supported international
standards, including: ANSI standards for terminals, printers and magtape,
Ethernet, X.25, X.400, X11, Motif, TCP/IP, ISO/OSI, IEEE POSIX, X/Open XPG.
             
Any operating system that conforms to the above standards is very open
because it can be used by a customer as a component in implementing very
vendor neutral applications.  For example, an application developed on such
a platform using X, Motif, and POSIX could be easily transferred to another
conformant platform with no change to the user interface and (almost?) no
change to the application.          


