In Article <294mn6$hn6@hpfcbig.sde.hp.com>  
marc@sde.hp.com (Marc Sabatella) writes:

>I think the relevant definition here - given the original context of the post,
>which was in response to someone's complaint that each vendor had their own
>variant of Unix - is the following:
>
>	An operating system is "open" if it is implemented by several vendors
>	for several different platforms.

Such a "definition" arise from losing sight of what the purpose of an open
system is.  Surely:

   An open system is one built by a customer that minimises vendor lock-in
   (to the extent that is appropriate to the customer).

Clearly Marc's "definition" is only one of many strategies that someone
might chose to adopt.  It would be useful for someone who wanted to keep
an identical operationg systems environment, but wants the freedom to run
on different hardware.  

However a more common requirement would be for application portability,
where the application can be run on more than one O/S and hardware
combination.  This requires conformance to appropraite standards so that
the application is portable between O/S's --- it does not require
portability of the O/S to multiple hardware.

      


