>gives you? I have seen some large conglomerations of Unix boxes that claim to
>be clusters but it appears it is necessary to have a complete copy of the OS
>for each machine...?

HP-UX runs client machines diskless if necessary.  You can have local swap
and file systems if you want to.  Works great.  Boots very fast.
No crashes in 5 years (except on day a user turned off the power
conditioner to a client, but a reboot reformed the cluster without
any problems)

Our old VMS cluster (780, 8xuVAXII, uVAXIII) used to take two
hours to boot, and used most of it's available power to just 
run the cluster, since DECnet is an active protocol, instead of
passive  (UNIX only knows of a failure when the remote machine 
fails to answer a request, VMS chatters incessantly, clogging up the
ETHERNET bandwidth).  It worked, but at a huge cost...  If another VAX
wanted to get the same advantages as our cluster (mainly shared
file systems) they couldn't, since file sharing wasn't available... :-(
I used to love when the VMS cluster would become two clusters
when quorum was lost.  Really funny!

