In article <1993Oct7.023037.18596@gordian.com>,
 mike@gordian.com (Michael Thomas) writes:
>   There are plenty of good things to be said about VMS, but
> flexibility through cheap process creation (and all it implies)
> is something that Unix spanks VMS's butt on. 

I wouldn't go as far as that, but $CREPRC *is* pretty slow by Unix fork()
standards.  

> Also, unix doesn't
> have the silly restrictions about the CLI running in supervisor
> mode, and the voodoo that is associated with it. You want DCL
> on Unix, just write it. Consing up csh for VMS is a much scarier
> proposition.

I don't see this at all.  You want a csh for VMS that stays in user mode and
$CREPRC's subprocesses (you don't have to use [LIB$]SPAWN, you know, and if
you're not running DCL commands in the subprocs there isn't much reason to) for
all commands?  Fine, there's nothing stopping you from doing that. There is
certainly nothing in RMS or in the VMS kernel that insists that a program that
behaves like a CLI has to run in supervisor mode, or has to run its code in P1
space, or anything like that.  (Last time I looked, you could invoke $CREPRC
from user mode...) 

That way you avoid all the supervisor mode "voodoo" (not that there's much to
it anyway). 

But... you may run into some "interesting" problems when it comes to sharing
your stdin and stdout between the CLI and the subprocesses it creates. Similar
for other files and devices.  That imo is what drove the VMS CLI into the
"share the process with the programs invoked by the CLI" design model.  There
are a *lot* of security issues relating to ownership and access to allocated
devices that just don't occur under Unix (mainly because the Unix kernel never
bothered its pretty little head about such things.  So you get horrors like the
use of "lock files" to arbitrate for access to not-inherently-shareable devices
like serial ports... and hope to Ghu that all code that wants those serial
ports knows enough to ask for the lock file first....). 

