    I have found very few variations of the the main UNIX commands. 
Sure there are few deviations like "ls -g" on System V and BSD (and
these differences go away with POSIX), but by and large, UNIX is as
compatible as any two versions of VMS at the user-level.  UNIX's
problem with incompabilities has been the stupid differences between
different vendors.  Plus, because UNIX is not a single vendor entity,
changes in the sematics/syntax of a system call/command are not
reflected immediately in every UNIX.  Does every DCL script written for
VMS 4.? work under VMS 5.5?

     If you stick within a single vendor, than VMS no longer has this
lock on consistency.  MVS is MVS, AOS is AOS, Ultrix is Ultrix, Foobar
is Foobar.  On the otherhand, in a hetrogenous UNIX "cluster" most of
the commands you already know still work and you can use them to figure
out whatever differences tare.  I mean, why UNICOS (UNIX for the Cray
supercomputers) and Univel (a PC UNIX) should be 100% identical is
beyond me...
    
