	In <CEJnK6.19I@dscomsa.desy.de> pawlak@zeubac.desy.de (Jerzy
	Michal Pawlak) writes:

	[Regarding ls -d in Unix:]
	
	>OK, so now, that we agreed that it is always -d can anybody
	>tell me WHY do I have to add -d to get the information I
	>need? After all, ls gives me information about a file and a
	>directory is a file? Don't you think it's a little
	>inconsistent? Demanding that for some files you add -d and
	>for others not?

Geez, aren't we getting a little petty?

"ls", when acting on a directory, defaults to listing the directory's
>contents<.  Just like "DIR" in VMS, when a directory is specified
with bracket notation (i.e. [.mydir]).  But if you want to list the
attributes for a Unix directory, you need to tell "ls" to do so with
the "-d" option.  In VMS you have to actually list the directory
>file< (i.e. [.topdir]mydir.dir).  VMS solves the problem by having a
seperate syntax for specifying directories and files, whereas Unix
treats files and directories in the same way for syntactic purposes.

If we want to nag about VMS: can anybody tell my WHY I have to
specificy the directory >file< when doing operations on it (like
changing protection, renaming it, etc.)?  Yet when I want to look
inside a directory on VMS, I have to use the bracket notation (e.g.
[DIR])?  Geez, in Unix I don't have to use different directory syntax
notations ;-)

For the humor-impaired, that previous paragraph was a joke.  But it
also points out something that is confusing in VMS (I know, because I
have fielded many questions on this point).  The "-d" option of "ls"
is also somewhat confusing (I know, because I have fielded questions
on this point as well).

Unix and VMS have different strengths and weaknesses.  Neither one is
better than the other ... they are just different OS's.

