> The real joke is "Open VMS". Open to what exactly?

Depends on what "open" means.  Nobody agrees.  Here are some definitions I've
seen, with comments:

o	Open = sources available.  Great!  DECsystem10s are open systems!

o	Open = second source for the operating system.  Could be -- what
	happens when you boot Ultrix on a Personal Iris?

o	Open = conforms to some collection of published standards.  This is
	what DEC seems to mean.  OpenVMS conforms to a number of Posix bits.
	So OpenVMS is open!

o	Open = code word for Unix(tm).  This is what Sun and AT&T had in mind,
	IMHO, and they hypnotized a lot of people into believing it. 
	Unfortunately the vendors were smart enough to start the treatment
	on upper management, so it works.

o	Open = not proprietary.  If you think that Unix is not proprietary,
	just try selling it!  Unix is the property of Novell, and the many
	variants are the property of various licensees.  I've never heard of
	a non-proprietary operating system.
