Mark H. Wood wrote:

> In article <CEHIoL.201@fc.hp.com>, chan@fc.hp.com (Channing Benson) writes:
> [deletia]
> > The real joke is "Open VMS". Open to what exactly?

> Depends on what "open" means.  Nobody agrees.

I agree that nobody agree :-)

I think the relevant definition here - given the original context of the post,
which was in response to someone's complaint that each vendor had their own
variant of Unix - is the following:

	An operating system is "open" if it is implemented by several vendors
	for several different platforms.

The very problem described about Unix - each vendor's implementation does "ls"
a little differently - cannot exist on VMS, simply because there is only only
VMS vendor.  Well, great.  If you limit yourself to a single Unix vendor, you
can get this exact same degree of "consistency".

Random comments: "ll" indeed does not exist everywhere; is an HP value added.
It is, however, effectively just an alias for "ls -l".  You can set up the same
thing on any Unix machine in the world in 5 seconds.

As for the complaints that the VMS vs Unix debate here has really just centered
on the shells, this isn't really true.  "ls" is not part of the shell.  It is
a standalone command.  And to the extent that the SVID (or POSIX, or X/OPEN,
etc) defines what is means to be "Unix", then gripes about "ls" syntax are
perfectly valid.  An OS is not just the kernel.

That said, though, I found the "dir" versus "ls" debate most amusing - all it
has proved to me is that both commands are rather cryptic.  It is funny to
see some people argue "dir [./dir] (or whatever) is obvious superior to ls
-ld .." and see people argue just the opposite.  The truth is, we like what
we're familiar with, and we can get used to just about everything.

Another comment was made to the effect that "it is good that VMS's backup
command takes all sorts of options to allow you to specify the set of files,
and bad that Unix makes you use find or ls and pipes, because the VMS way needs
only one process [and also, you only have to look one place in the manual -
ed.]"  Well, this is true, but there is also a tradeoff - requiring each
program to include its own parsing requires each program to include parsing
code.  Shareable libraries help reduce the most obvious sources of duplication
of effort, but it is nice to be able to use creative means of file
specification that the program designer never thought of, and pipeline
structures are by far the easiest way to do this.

--
Marc Sabatella
marc@fc.sde.hp.com
