I've heard this stuff about spawning under VMS being slower than Unix
a lot.  I've worked on VMS systems for quite awhile and I guess I
don't understand where this comes from with today's fast CPUs - CPUs
which, by the way, are as fast or faster than most of the Unix CPUs
I've worked on.

There are many things that affect the speed of spawning on a VMS system,
and I hardly think it fair to compare the spawn speed on a VAX 11/780,
or even a VAX6000-410, with the speed of creating child processes on
a Unix system that can be as much as 8 times faster.

Another thing I've noticed is that a child process in Unix doesn't
seem to inherit any of the parent process' environment variables.  Well
heck!  I can spawn a subprocess on VMS such that symbols, logicals &
keypad definitions don't get copied to the subprocess' environment... and
guess what!  That spawn works *extremely* quickly, even on a 6000-410!

Maybe I'm not doing something quite right on the Unix side to copy the
environment variables from the parent process.  All I know is that if
I use "!" to create a subprocess, when using this newsreader (or even
mail) on the Unix system, my parent process' environment variables do
not get copied.  The difference in speed of doing this on a Unix system
compared to a VAX 6000-410 isn't even measurable to me (and I'm using my
trusty-dusty, neo-atomic, laser time displacement mechanism that has
precision down to 1.0342454 x 10^-435983745 of a second ;-).
