ls -d works on SunOS 4.1.3, HP-UX 9.0, and IBM's AIX 3.2. I don't know
about other UNIXes because I don't have them. You're right that UNIX
isn't the same from one system to another, but this one wasn't such a
great example.

Regardless, whenever people start arguing about OSes, they always
argue about the shell syntax. A shell is just a program, and there's
no reason someone couldn't write their own shell for UNIX (or VMS, I
presume) that does things completely differently.

A better comparison would involve the underlying facilities of each
operating system. In this case, VMS has its advantages over UNIX in
some areas, while UNIX wins out in others. It all depends on which
software products run on which platform, which in turn depends on what
you want to do.

