Maybe you should provide *your* definitions of "open" and "proprietary".
Do you think Exabyte is "open"?

Open, to me, means that it can work with a variety of other hardware.
In other words, a SCSI-based 1/2 tape drive from DEC is just as open
as an 8-mm drive from Exabyte.  The cost may be just a bit more, but
when I backup my data, I *have to* guarantee that I can restore it.
Cheaper 8-mm drives (& even 4-mm) drives are just too unreliable - from
both a hardware and a media standpoint.

Everything worth anything is "proprietary".  That's what drives
businesses to improve their products.  In fact, I'd pick proprietary
products over so-called "open" (or industry-standard) "products" any
day.  Leave the open standards to the architectural definitions.  I want
products that will be improved often enough that I don't have to live
with technology so old it can't keep up with all the other pieces of a
"modern" system (e.g., QIC).

The terms "Open Products" and "Standard Products" sound too much like
the broken-promises of communism to me.
