What you can do is to modify the termcap entry for your vt102 on your system
to enable padding.  vt100 terminals usually need extensive padding at high
speeds.  Padding in a termcap entry is done by inserting a number between the
equals sign and the command code in the termcap string capability.  Basically
what padding is is that when the host system sends out a command string, it
immediately follows the string with the number of padding characters specified
in the termcap entry.  The pad character is usually a null "ascii zero".  If
you have never had to pad a termcap entry, buy and read the book "Termcap &
Terminfo" by O'Reilly & Associates, Inc. which contains an extensive
description and explanation of it.
  Incidentally, sometimes terminal manufacturers can supply you with the correct
padding numbers, but usually they won't as it is embarressing to admit that your
terminal needs to be padded.

