Ummm - you might try ps aux , without the -.  That happens to work on ULTRIX,
and will work identically on DEC OSF/1.  If you look at the ps man page, DEC
OSF/1 uses the  - to differentiate between system V and BSD modes on ps.
So, for ULTRIX, SUN users, ps aux will do what you expect.  For System V
users, ps -ef will also do the right thing.

>btw I havn't seen anyone mention clustering in this thread yet? Is there any
>Unix cluster technology out there that comes anywhere close to what VMSCluster
>gives you? I have seen some large conglomerations of Unix boxes that claim to
>be clusters but it appears it is necessary to have a complete copy of the OS
>for
>each machine...?

Actually, MANY UNIX implementations support totally DISKLESS configurations.
Between NFS, Automounter, NIS, and the upcoming DCE DFS stuff, UNIX today can
provide  a very large subset of the VMS clustering capability.  The major
advantage that VMS still has is the distributed lock manager that allows 
multiple concurrent physical access to the disks.  That's a neat trick, and
UNIX has a way to go duplicate it.


