> not get copied.  The difference in speed of doing this on a Unix system
> compared to a VAX 6000-410 isn't even measurable to me (and I'm using my
> trusty-dusty, neo-atomic, laser time displacement mechanism that has
> precision down to 1.0342454 x 10^-435983745 of a second ;-).

When one forks a child process in Unix one inherits all these goodies from
the parent:
		real, effective, and saved user ID
		real, effective, and saved group ID
		list of supplementary group IDs (see getgroups(2))
		process group ID
		environment
		file descriptors
		close-on-exec flags (see exec(2))
		signal handling settings (SIG_DFL, SIG_IGN, address)
		signal mask (see sigvector(2))
		profiling on/off status (see profil(2))
		command name in the accounting record (see acct(4))
		nice value (see nice(2))
		all attached shared memory segments (see shmop(2))
		current working directory
		root directory (see chroot(2))
		file mode creation mask (see umask(2))
		file size limit (see ulimit(2))
		real-time priority (see rtprio(2))

There  are  variations  on the  exec  function  which  can  replace  the
inherited  environment  with a new one for execed process.  I think this
is how a shell "exports" environment strings to its children.  Maybe the
fork/exec  shell escape for your programs use this variation to wipe out
the environment.

