> In article <1993Oct7.023037.18596@gordian.com> mike@gordian.com (Michael Thomas) writes:
>>  Er, need I point out the difference between lib$spawn() and fork()?
>>Unix piping etc, etc, is *painfully* slow under VMS. Believe me,
>>I've tried and it takes *much* hacking to get the VMS side to
>>behave respectably.
> 
> Even though VMS sucks, it *is* possible to get reasonable performance with
> a spawn in VMS, if you use SPAWN/NOSYM/NOLOG to stop the DCL thingy from
> copying all those zillions of bytes of tables down to the new process.
> What you see is VMS's lack of the path concept, which is why UNIX is faster
> on the spawn...  Of course, your subprocess will have to find another
> way to locate all those VMS goodies, like SYS$WHATCHAMACALLIT :-)

                                            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

        Close, but no cigar.  It is  only your PROCESS logicals that don't
    get  copied  to  the subprocess on SPAWN/NOLOGICAL_NAMES.   There  are
    _many_ shared logical name tables, LNM$JOB and LNM$SYSTEM to name two,
    that get you to essentially everything you need.  Logical names  in  a
    shared  lnm  table  do  NOT  get  "copied"  to  the subprocess but are
    available to it.  In particular,  anything  named  SYS$xxx is going to
    reside  in  a  directory  pointed  to  by  a  logical  in  LNM$SYSTEM.
    "Private" logical names that you want the subprocess to inherit simply
    need to be defined /JOB in the user's LOGIN.COM  rather  than  in  the
    default  LNM$PROCESS.  (BTW, /NOLOG is not quite sufficient...it needs
    to be /NOLOGI  since  there  is  also  a  /NOLOG (/LOG) qualifier that
    controls  whether certain informational messages are displayed at  the
    time of the SPAWN.)

        As another poster mentioned, the /NOSYMBOLS can be a big win since
    most users...and system  managers  on  behalf  of their users...define
    _lots_ of symbols which need to be copied to the child process.  

        Just trying to take care of factual details while staying clear of
    the gun fire...

            -Ken
