>   OK, so now, that we agreed that it is always -d can anybody tell me
>   WHY do I have to add -d to get the information I need? After all, ls 
>   gives me information about a file and a directory is a file? Don't you
>   think it's a little inconsistent? Demanding that for some files you add
>   -d and for others not?

I can tell you why.  Because it is much more often the case that you
want to look at the files *in* a directory than at the directory
itself (at least *I* find this to be the case).  In VMS, you have to
specify the brackets and the dot to do what is the common case rather
than the special case.  Similarly, to copy a file into a directory,
you just specify the directory name as the target.  If the target
directory doesn't exist, you don't get an error -- you just end up
copying the file to a new filename in the current directory.  I found
these things annoying and non-intuitive when I started using Unix (I
used VMS for a few years before I was exposed to Unix).  But now that
I'm used to them, they make a lot of sense, and allow me to type
commands a lot faster.  So, I've used both VMS and U*ix, and I prefer
U*ix.  I also like Macintoshes more than PCs.  To each his own I
guess.

	Steve
