In article <1993Oct5.114827.95@flying-disk.com>, frisbie@flying-disk.com writes...
> 
>What is the interrupt priority of the KWV11-SF (same board
>as the KWV11-C) real time clock board?
> 
>The Unibus equivalent, the KW11-P, was shipped at priority
>6, but could be jumpered for 4, 5, 6, or 7.   The KWV11
>does not appear to have such an option.

According to my Q-bus SPEC, there are 4 IRQ lines, and 1 acknowledge line
(unlike the Unibus). To interrupt, you always assert BIRQ4, and other signals
as follows.
Level      Signals
4          BIRQ4
5          BIRQ4,BIRQ5
6          BIRQ4,BIRQ6
7          BIRQ4,BIRQ6,BIRQ7

The original LSI11 processor did not have multiple interrupt levels, and always
responded to BIRQ4. 
You could try looking to see which (if any) of the pins for these signals have
tracks connected to them. BIRQ4 must have, and by what others (if any) also
have tracks, you can work out what the priority is likely to be.

> 
>Unfortunately, none of the KWV11 documents disclose the
>priority that DEC used.

I couldn't find it either...

