1. I work already for 10 years in VMS (sysadmin, programmer, ...) and of course
then you create a special relationship with that operating system (which you 
also would do if you work for 10 years with any other OS). Since about a year, 
I work (altough not intensively) with UNIX and of course the command interfaceS
(!!!) are much more cryptic (I compare it with MCR of RSX) but there are
features which VMS has not, and I like these (piping, redirection). Unix
command lines are more compact and therefor less easy to understand (but this
is the same experience a Fortran programmer has when he switches to C).

Altough I still like VMS much much more than Unix, WE NEED NOT ONLY OPEN
SYSTEMS, BUT ALSO OPEN MINDS. 

2. A question. In VMS file specifications you can include the network
information, which allows you to use all file related commands to access a
file anywhere. E.g. you just need COPY to copy files from here to there, even
over the network. Does such a facility also exists in Unix ? In other words, do
the developpers of unifiedUnix have in their minds a method to put the network
information in the file specification ? This would enhance the use of ftp if it
could be incorporated in commands like cp, rm, ls.
