In article <1993Oct5.201109.777@eisner.decus.org>, killeen@eisner.decus.org writes:
> Terry does this mean for things like 4mm tapes 3rd parties doing TMSCP are
> better off reporting the tape as a TK50 rather than a TU81? 

  I don't think so. The controller vendor should do whatever gyrations are
needed to provide the full TMSCP command set on the host side and make ef-
ective use of the bus/drive on the SCSI side. Otherwise, you'll get things
like a TMSCP "skip file" doing skips by tape marks and going very slowly.

  Unfortunately, most vendors implement whatever is needed to get by under
some OS (usually VMS) and leave it at that. When you try another OS, you get
problems. I know some folks (you included 8-) who have had good luck with
CMD controllers under RSTS/E. I run one on a VAX and it seems to work fine
as well.

