Jan Vorbrueggen (jan@neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de) wrote:

| Ah yes, file name syntax.  At least VMS allows me to look _at_ a
| directory, not only _into_ it.  Anytime I want to look at, say, the
| protection of a directory file under Unix, I have to do a _full_ listing
| of the parent directory (with all the resource wastage that entails),
| and grep for wanted file(s) in the output.  So in this case, the shell's
| piping capability come to the rescue...  And how do I find out how much
| space is taken up by a subset of files?

  "ll -d directory" will show information about a directory instead of
its contents.

  "wc -c *.h" will show the character counts in all "*.h" files and a
total.  To see just the total you could use "wc -c *.h | tail -1".


Marc Verbruggen (mvbr@se.alcbel.be) wrote:

| 2.  A question.  In VMS file specifications you can include the network
| information, which allows you to use all file related commands to access
| a file anywhere.  E.g.  you just need COPY to copy files from here to
| there, even over the network.  Does such a facility also exists in Unix?
| In other words, do the developpers of unifiedUnix have in their minds a
| method to put the network information in the file specification?  This
| would enhance the use of ftp if it could be incorporated in commands
| like cp, rm, ls.

  There have been notations like that used for some Unix "networking"
implementations.  The somewhat out of date uucp system featured a uux
command that accepted system prefixes before files and commands.  Here
is an extract from the uux manual entry.

|    uux a!cut -f1 b!/usr/file \(c!/usr/file\)
|  gets /usr/file from system b and sends it to system a, performs a cut
|  command on the file, and sends the result of the cut command to system c.

  NFS is currently the most common method for Unix networking.  This
tool mounts a directory tree from one machine onto a directory of
another machine's file system.  This can be used in a visible way by
mounting remote file systems on /nfs/sysname and refering to remote
files like "cp /nfs/sysA/usr/file /nfs/sysB/usr/file".  It can be used
in a much less visible way by making arbitrary directories like
/users/mvbr refer to directories on remote machines.

  I think that putting networking features in the file system using the
existing file system notation is a much better approach than putting
networking into commands with a distinct networking notation.  With
network file access done in the file system all programs can have
transparent access to network resources.

--
Mike Stroyan, mike_stroyan@fc.hp.com


