>|>writes:
>|>
>|>[Attribution Missing] writes:
>|>> If you had read the manual, you would have found out the -d option:
>|>> ll -d dirname gives you what you want (at least in HPUX)
>|>                                         ----------------
>|>
>|>                                        [   Exactly!   ;-]
>|>
>yeah and what is that option called in OSF/1, Ultrix, SunOs...?
>You'll be lucky if its the same ! Call *nix a standard? What a joke.

Actually, it's the same on all of those systems.  What is the equivalent
of DIR/SEC on HP VMS....  Oh, you mean VMS isn't available on HP?  Some
standard...  (Note that I know the preceding is a cheap shot, but so was
the "*nix a standard" comment.)

Most of the widely-used *NIX utilities do in fact have pretty standard
sets of options, and where compatibility problems are frustrating, the
GNU tools do an admirable job of enforcing de facto option standards
simply by being available in highly portable source code.

Now, I'm not claiming UNIX options aren't cryptic.  They usually are.
There are also two flavors of system utilities (Sys V-derived and BSD-
derived) which can create pain when you go from one type of system
to another until you learn both.

VMS is much easier for the casual user to get going with.  If you don't
really *know* the tools and haven't mastered the art of creative use of
apropos (or man -k) and man, you're going to find it frustrating.  Once
you've mastered the tools, though, you can do incredibly complex things
with very little effort.  On VMS, once you start banging into the walls
of what it's designed to do easily, you start having to write code to
use QIOs and RMS, needing to hand-massage directory listings into command
sequences (without the benefit of regular expression substitutions), or
having to write long nasty DCL scripts which generate other DCL scripts
to do something essentially straightforward....  And don't get me started
on DCL's quoting conventions.

VMS is great when you're doing the things it helps you do.  It just doesn't
help you do as many different things as *NIX does.  The problem with *NIX
is that it takes a lot of work to learn what it really does for you.

Anyway, these are OS religion issues, so I won't bother arguing further.
Suffice it to say that I am not VMS illiterate.  I did a lot of work on
the VAX C RTL when I worked for DEC, including a major rewrite of the I/O
system (which I think  may finally have shipped, only 3 years after the
initial work was done...  VMS release scheduling is another annoying thing,
though I understand the reasons it can be so glacially slow at times...)
I definitely understand the appeal of VMS.  I just find *NIX much more
flexible and better suited to my style and purposes.

- Brad
