>>Trashcans under OSF1 have major problems in that
>>	
>>	1) they are not inherited by subdirectories
>>	2) they are plauged by file nameing problems

		is #2 above in reference to the caveat that
		if you attach more than one directory to the
		same trashcan you can get file name collisions?

		one way that advfs could avoid this problem is
		by tagging the files in a trashcan with the original
		file's parent directory's inode number.  then there
		would not be file name collisions.  we'll look into it.

>>
>>Is it feasable to change advfs such that:
>>	
>>	a) calls to mkdir, or some lower system call will check if
>>	the parent directory has a trash can, and inheriets this attribute?
>>
		yes this is feasible.  however, others have argued
		against this approach because:

		1) now you have multiple directories attached to the
		   same trashcan and can have file name collisions.
		   the argument is that it is better to force the user
		   to make all trashcan attachments manually so they
		   are aware of how their trashcans are setup.

		2) not all directories need trashcans.  if mkdir did
		   the attachment then you would waste disk space
		   with files in the trashcan the you are not interested
	           in recovering.

		3) system admins cringe at the thought of having automatic
	           trashcans because they increase the amount of disk space
		   used by trashcans.

		you can always create a mkdir script that does the
		automatic attachment of the trashcan directory.


>>	b) allow trashcans to be set up as a subdirectory of a current 
>>	directory, such that:
>>
>>		mktrashcan -R trashcan ${HOME}

		how about:

			find . -depth -type d -exec mt2 {} \;

		where mt2 is a script containing the following (there is
		probably a way to do this without a script):

			mkdir $1/trashcan
			mktrashcan $1/trashcan $1
		
>>	setsup a trashcan subdirectory in each of directories from
>>	${HOME} on down, ties each directory to its trashcan subdirectory?
>>
>>
>>this scheme seems to work well togather, particularly if you tie it togather 
>>with a cleanup proceedure for cron that will remove files after so much time 
>>from all subdirectories called trashcan. The only real change to the os is to
>>have the mkdir sys call check if the parent has a trashcan ... 


	basically, the idea behind the mktrashcan command is to
	provide basic (low-level) support for trashcans.  that way
	it is very flexible in how you can use it to setup trashcans.

	it was difficult to predict how people will want to use the
	trashcan feature so we made it as simple (and low-level) as
	possible so that it would be useful (especially if used in
	conjuction with other commands like cron and find).

	we'll take your suggestions into consideration for the next
	version.

	thanks,

	pete...


