Well, your article and flame are about as useless as tits on a bird.

The original poster was having problems with floating-point byte ordering,
so let's show him the nature of the problem.

Enclosed is an extract from an article I posted a year or so ago which
illustrates the "problem" on VAX (DEC) architectures contrasted to HP, AT&T,
Amiga, Apple, SGI, and Sun systems.  Though my example uses "double", the
problem with "float" is similar.  Besides which, DEC doesn't use the STANDARD
IEEE floating-point representation across-the-board by default on all its
platforms (if any).

In Silicon Valley, Intel is known as "First With the Worst", so the number of
machines using Intel's (and DEC's) byte-ordering as found on IBM-PCs (and
clones thereof) is irrelevant to the current discussion (though I do
understand the original reason for "little endian" memory data/instruction
organization (cost of memory back in the 1960s and 1970s)).

Thad Floryan [ thad@btr.com, thad@cup.portal.com, thad@netcom.com ]

---------- begin enclosure

/*	showres - display floating point numbers on architecture
 */

#include <stdio.h>
#include <sys/utsname.h>

typedef union {
	double	dbl_num;
	struct {
	   long	lw0;
	   long	lw1;
	} Ints;
} DBL;

DBL		foo;
struct utsname	arch;

main()
{
	uname(&arch);
	printf("Sys=%s, node=%s, release=%s, version=%s, machine=%s\n",
	arch.sysname, arch.nodename, arch.release, arch.version, arch.machine);

	foo.dbl_num = 1.0;		showme();
	foo.dbl_num = 10.0;		showme();
	foo.dbl_num = 100.0;		showme();
	foo.dbl_num = 1.23456789;	showme();
	foo.dbl_num = 12.3456789;	showme();
	foo.dbl_num = 123.456789;	showme();
}

showme()
{
printf("\t%20.10f = %08x, %08x\n", foo.dbl_num, foo.Ints.lw0, foo.Ints.lw1);
}


