Daryl Morse (morse@quark.mpr.ca) wrote:

>>first part of post deleted<<

: Also, I am wondering about the Fast Wide SCSI interface as opposed to
: the Fast SCSI interface. It's my understanding that DEC OSF/1 V1.3
: currently supports Fast SCSI, but not Fast Wide SCSI. Supposedly the
: next version of DEC OSF/1 will support Fast Wide SCSI. Will Fast Wide
: SCSI devices default to the Fast SCSI mode if the system doesn't
: support it? I'd like to order a Fast Wide SCSI device if possible to
: take advantage of the extra speed when it's supported. Are there any
: potential problems with that?

Daryl,

  The issue of Fast Wide SCSI is more an issue of hardware at this point.
There are issues of signal integrity to be watched for any Fast SCSI product
(wide or not) that doesn't use differential signalling. Most of the current
SCSI market uses single ended signalling technology (that is not to say that
you can't physically "make" a Fast Single Ended SCSI function, but that I
would be very careful about understanding such a product and it's warranties
prior to using it). The base level is that you can't mix a single ended 
device in with differential devices. And, you need a Fast Wide SCSI controller
that uses differential signalling (sorry for being so long winded).

  I believe that GENROCO has a TURBOchannel Fast Wide Differential SCSI card
that works under Alpha OSF. They also have an IPI controller, you can look
at the price differentials (not a lot, if I remember correctly) versus the
performance differentials from their product literature.

  I have never worked with the GENROCO FWD SCSI hardware, but I have some
familiarity with their IPI product line. It seems like their product would be
worth your investigation. They're the only ones shipping a TURBOchannel FWD
SCSI product that I know of. You can reach them at   sales@genroco.com   for
a quote and product information. This is all from memory, but I'm pretty sure
it's right.

I hope that helps.

ciao,
 aj


