In article <28je36$sh3@news.delphi.com>, bytehed@news.delphi.com (BYTEHED@DELPHI.COM) writes:
> I have a few questions concerning a VAX 11/750. I recently acquired this
> beasty, and spent the alloted time connecting the cables and what not. I
> then attempted to boot it, but the unit wouldn't boot. I found that the
> motor was severly "warped", so a kind soul on these nets sent a
> replacement. Well, I installed the motor, and the unit still won't boot. I
> get the message "RD Fault" on the front console. I also noticed that the
> unit attempts to access the RL02 drive while booting too. So my questions
> boil down to this:

  "RD Fault" is supposed to come on for a short while during the power-up
sequence. You need to attach a console terminal to the console port on the
back of the cabinet (it's a DB25). There may be a second DB25 there for the
remote diagnostic option, so you may have to experiment.

  The console should display messages which will help us solve the problem.
If you have the latest ROMs _and_ the PCS patch tape in the little TU58 tape
drive in the front of the cabinet, the console should print:

%%
@@

  as it loads the PCS patches. After that, what happens depends on the set-
ting of the boot switch on the front panel (older systems) or on the default
boot file on the TU58 tape (newer systems).

> #1. What should be my next course of action

  See above.

> #2. Does the RL02 require a Disk Pack mounted for the unit to boot?

  If you're trying to boot from the RL02, yes. What other disk(s) do you have?
What operating system do you think you have on the disk(s)?

> #3. How do I garner any visual output from this unit? I see the array
>     of DB25 ports. How do I interface it with my DECpc 466d2? Or 
>     should I purchase a dedicated terminal?

  See above.

> #4. I am going to recheck the motor mounting, and make sure it is
>     mounted properly, but assuming it is, could the HDA be defective?

  What disk are we talking about here? It's not an RL02, as RL02's have packs,
not HDA's. If it's an RA81, yes, it's possible that the HDA is bad. However,
we need to see what you get on the console terminal first.

> #5. What is the make date, power, and usage of this unit? Capabilities
>     , maximum operation. It has 8 1 meg boards in it.

  It's probably from around 1982. It is rated at .6 VUP, which makes it 2/3
the speed of a MicroVAX II. It's still supported under VMS and 4.3BSD Unix,
and probably some other stuff as well. It has a 30A single-phase plug, but
pulls less than 10A (CPU alone, no disks). You can get a MS750-HB upgrade to
take the system to 14Mb, the architectural maximum for that system.

  By today's standards this system is big and slow. You might be better off
with a MicroVAX II. On the other hand, you already have this system, so you
may as well fix it up.

  I see you're in NJ - so am I (other end of the state, though). If you want,
send me private email and maybe I can come look at your system - I have a
complete set of spare parts, etc.

