In article <1993Oct3.231103.14693@decus.com.au>, geremin@decus.com.au writes:
> Hello RSTS Users where-ever you are, Quick Question from Down Under.

  We're all over. It's the protective coloration that fools you 8-)

> Whilst at the DECUS-93 Symposium last month in Adelaide, I got a
> copy of the recent? RSTS/E v10.1 SPD from the nice Digital man.
> 
> It does not mention support for any SCSI devices. Why NOT ?

  At the time V10.1 shipped, the RQZX1 had not completed qualification testing.
[That's DECspeak for "the OS support is done but the board is still flaky 8-]
Thus, it isn't mentioned in the SPD. I believe the cover letter for the 10.1
kit says "Ignore references to the RQZX1 and RZ-series disks until we tell you
otherwise".

> My initial thought was that there should not need to be any changes to 
> the PDP-11 operating systems if the I/F does a good MSCP implementation, 
> but I heard that RT-11, RSX and RSTS would need to be upgraded to use SCSI.

  The board does other things besides SCSI - it supports the RX33 floppy, etc.
In the case of RSTS/E, some issues are the controller's MSCP model number,
handling of tape errors (remember, not all operations legal on a TU-81 [the
"primordial" TMSCP device] are available on the SCSI TZ30. This is similar to
the "skip file" having to be implemented as "skip -1 records" for the TK50 a
while back). This is complicated by some issues in the TZ30 itself.

> What is the real story?  Is it ready to go - if so why no mention
> of SCSI disks in the SPD ?  If NOT why the delay - what is the problem?

  My understanding is that qualification was completed and it is now supported.
However, all the disks supported at the time of the initial announcement have
been discontinued (RZ23, RZ24) and at the time DEC didn't want to commit to
supporting the larger disks. Unless a shop needs an all-DEC solution for polit-
ical reasons or needs RX33 support, 3rd-party controllers such as those from
CMD cost less and have no restrictions on "supported" disk sizes. I know some
customers running multiple > 1Gb drives and DAT tapes on the CMD's under RSTS/E
V10.0 and V10.1 with no problems.

  Lastly, I have heard that all PDP-11 software engineering groups have been
shut down, with support coming from the Customer Support Centers (or is that
Centres? 8-). Nobody I know has seen a Software Dispatch in *ages*, at least.

